PROPOSAL — not shipped, not applied by `weld add cron`.

Target: capabilities/loom/files/di.go.tmpl (regenerated internal/di/di.go and
internal/di/loom_gen.go)
Owner: the loom capability.

Why it is not applied here: di.go is owned by the loom capability, and its
capability-aware `{{if .Caps.Has "..."}}` blocks are regenerated on every
`weld add`. cron must not edit it.

Proposed graph changes, mirroring how redis declares an available binding:

1. Add a provider behind a capability guard:

    {{- if .Caps.Has "cron"}}
    // The scheduler is defined in cron_provider.go, the stable seam.
    loom.Provide(NewScheduler),
    {{- end }}

2. Make the graph root consume it, so Loom constructs the scheduler (an
   unreferenced provider is pruned and its lifecycle hooks never append):

    type App struct {
        Config *config.Config
        Server *Server
    {{- if .Caps.Has "cron"}}
        Scheduler *cron.Scheduler
    {{- end}}
    }

    func NewApp(cfg *config.Config, server *Server{{if .Caps.Has "cron"}}, scheduler *cron.Scheduler{{end}}) *App {
        return &App{Config: cfg, Server: server{{if .Caps.Has "cron"}}, Scheduler: scheduler{{end}}
    }

3. Ship a stable provider seam, written once by cron when loom is also
   installed (like redis_provider.go), e.g. internal/di/cron_provider.go:

    package di

    import (
        "context"
        "log/slog"

        "__module__/internal/config"
        "__module__/internal/cron"
        "github.com/Xwudao/loom"
    )

    // NewScheduler builds the application scheduler and registers its start and
    // stop with the Loom lifecycle, so serve starts it and graceful shutdown
    // stops it. It validates the cron timezone when the graph constructs it.
    func NewScheduler(lc *loom.Lifecycle, cfg *config.Config, logger *slog.Logger) (*cron.Scheduler, error) {
        location, err := cfg.Cron.Location()
        if err != nil {
            return nil, err
        }
        scheduler := cron.New(logger, cron.WithLocation(location))
        lc.Append(loom.Hook{
            OnStart: func(ctx context.Context) error {
                scheduler.Start(ctx)
                return nil
            },
            OnStop: func(ctx context.Context) error {
                return scheduler.Stop(ctx)
            },
        })
        return scheduler, nil
    }

`*loom.Lifecycle` is already a provider dependency (NewServer takes it), so this
needs no new Loom feature. Jobs are registered by making another provider (or
the App root, after InitApp) depend on *cron.Scheduler and call Register; there
is no config-declared job list, so `weld add cron` still schedules nothing.
